 Raising the threshold from 50 GeV to 70 GeV reduced the number of selected values from 5 to 3 because fewer particles have energies above a higher cut. 
 The histogram of the original data does not depend on the threshold so only the red line moves and the bars stay the same.
 With 5 bins (20 GeV wide) the histogram is coarse and shows only the overall spread of energies while 20 bins (5 GeV wide) show more detail.
 With only ten values, the 20-bin histogram is mostly empty bins with one or zero entries so its shape is dominated by statistical noise rather than a real distribution.
With thousands of events, the fine binning would give a smooth, meaningful shape and the choice of binning would matter much less than it does here
